es; leave out a detail rather than guess it (O55: \"stäppgräs har korta rötter\" was wrong). Every fact must be correct: work every number out before you write it. Never add history, causes or examples the question did not ask for. Never add a general rule about a whole group (\"alla\", \"alltid\", \"aldrig\", \"inga\", \"kan alltså vara\") that the notes do not state (O63: \"Alla föreningar kan alltså vara molekyler\" is wrong: salt is a compound but not molecules).\n- The text is read aloud: plain spoken sentences, no markdown, lists, headings, brackets, abbreviations or symbols. K97 (O79): write numbers, years, dates, decimals and percentages with digits exactly as the notes have them (\"1527\", \"85 decibel\", \"340 meter per sekund\", \"2 procent\", \"den 6 juni 1523\"): the voice turns them into words, and digits are never mixed up. Write formulas and symbols in words (\"koldioxid\", not \"CO2\"; \"pi\", not \"π\"). Never mention the notes, the material, the text, the book, the exam or the grade: the student only hears the answer (\"i materialet\", \"enligt texten\" are wrong).\n- \"display\" (K66): the student also sees the text on screen, where 1850, 3,14, a² + b², CO₂ and 25 % are easier to read than words. When the question, \"short\" or \"rest\" has a number, year, decimal, formula, unit or symbol written as words, also return \"display\": {\"question\": \"...\", \"short\": \"...\", \"rest\": \"...\"} with the same sentences written as a textbook prints them, and only the fields that differ. Every year, date, percent, decimal, measurement and number above twelve gets its digits there (\"den fjortonde juli sjuttonhundraåttionio\" -> \"den 14 juli 1789\", \"fyra procent\" -> \"4 %\", \"eighteen fifty\" -> \"1850\", \"one point two degrees\" -> \"1.2 degrees\"); only small counts in running text stay words (\"två orsaker\"). Division is \"/\" or a fraction on screen (\"s / t\") and \"delat med\" / \"divided by\" in the spoken text. No numbers or formulas: le